{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Because he afflicted.</b> It should have said 'because he lay with,' for if he had to force her, then she is an unwilling party and exempt — and if so, how does Scripture obligate her with stoning? Rather, surely it was with her consent. And the reason it used the language of ענה is to say: we reckon him as one who forces, in order to condemn him to stoning, and we reckon the woman as consenting, to obligate her with death — since she was in the city and could have cried out but did not cry out; and we reckon the man as forcing and compelling her, since he did not seduce her beforehand, and we obligate him with death. And the fact that Scripture mentions 'city' and 'field' is not exclusive, for if she committed adultery in the city with her consent she is stoned, and if she was forced in the field she is exempt — since Scripture spoke only of the usual case, that coercion is common in the field and not in the city. And certainly if she was forced even in the city she is exempt, and if she committed adultery willingly in the field she is liable, and everything depends on the testimony of the witnesses."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:25.365388446Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}